// The FSE sequence decoder's fused table — **not redesigned, and the reason is the interesting one.**
//
// `packages/zstd/src/sequences.wac`'s `Fused` is the seventh thing this exercise has decided to
// leave alone, and the first where the reason is that **the platform, not the language, is what
// forces the shape.**
//
// ## What it does, and why it is right
//
// Its own account, and the numbers are in it:
//
// > Decoding one code used to be five loads … Three codes per sequence made thirteen loads to
// > produce three numbers, and the sequence loop is over half of decode time. … one `info` word plus
// > one baseline, indexed by state directly. Six loads per sequence rather than thirteen. This is
// > what zstd's own decoder does.
//
// Fusing five arrays into two is a layout decision with a measured reason and a citation to the
// reference implementation. Nothing here improves it.
//
// ## The half that is a workaround, and cannot stop being one
//
// `info` is three fields in an `i32`:
//
//     info[s] = (codeBits[c] << 24) | (t.nbBits[s] << 16) | t.newState[s];
//
// with the proof that they fit written in prose: *"extra bits reach 31 (offsets), state bit counts
// reach the accuracy log of 9, and a next-state base is under 512."* Three widths, argued in a
// comment, checked by nothing.
//
// The shape that says it is `struct Entry { u8 extraBits; u8 nbBits; u16 newState; }` and an
// `Entry[]` — **and that is slower, not faster.** A WasmGC array of a struct type is an array of
// *references*: one indirection per read, in the loop this file exists to take indirections out of.
// The packing is not a workaround for a missing language feature. It is a workaround for a missing
// **runtime** feature, and WasmGC has no third option — an array's elements are packed primitives
// (`i8`, `i16`) or references, and never inline structs.
//
// So `@/packages/bls`'s trade does not apply here. There the wrapper cost one `struct.new` per
// operation, bounded at 0.7% by numbers already measured; here it would cost one dereference per
// *read*, three per sequence, thousands of sequences per block, in over half of decode time.
//
// ## What a language could still do, which is smaller and is not nothing
//
// It cannot make `Entry[]` fast. It can make the packing **checked**:
//
//     packed struct Entry { u8 extraBits; u8 nbBits; u16 newState; }   // lowers to one i32
//
// — a declaration that names the three fields, fixes their widths, and compiles every read to the
// shift and mask the file writes by hand. The prose proof becomes the declaration; a fourth field
// does not silently overflow into the third; and `info[s] >> 24` stops being a number a reader has
// to check against a comment four screens up.
//
// This is **not** `issues/lang/0074`. That issue is about values with no identity *exploded into
// locals* — its evidence is ChaCha20 at 4.7x and `packages/bls` at −64%, both from moving array
// elements into registers, and its crux is *"the spec text has to say the compiler is required to
// keep these in locals"*. An array of a hundred thousand entries cannot be in locals. Same want,
// different lowering, and 0074's answer does not reach this case. `../../../QUESTIONS.md`.

// Nothing is rewritten below. The file is here for the note above and for the shape of the entry it
// argues about.

/** Three fields in an `i32`, with the widths in a comment — see the header. */
export struct Fused {
  i32[] info;
  i32[] base;
}
